\section{Which allele becomes 1}{

By default (\code{method = "frequency"}) the \strong{most frequent allele is the
reference}: it is coded \code{1}, the minor allele \code{-1}, and the heterozygote
\code{0}. The choice is made per marker from the observed genotype calls, so it
does not depend on how the input file ordered its alleles -- with one
exception, the tie rule below.

\strong{Tie rule (minor-allele frequency exactly 0.5).} When the two homozygote
classes are equally frequent at a marker (for example 50 of 100 doubled-haploid
lines carry each allele), no allele is more frequent, and the coding follows
the allele order instead: the first-listed allele ("allele 1") is coded as the
reference (\code{1}, or \code{2} under \code{code_as = "012"}), and the second-listed as the
minor allele (this is the strict \code{>} comparison in the numericalization
kernel: the alleles are swapped only when the second allele is strictly more
frequent). "First-listed" means the first allele of the input's allele pair:
\code{REF} for VCF, the first allele of the \code{alleles} column for HapMap, \code{A1} (as
SNPRelate lists it) for PLINK bed/ped, and the first allele of \code{snp.allele} in
a GDS file. For inputs that declare no allele pair (nucleotide tables and
Illumina FinalReport) the alphabetically first allele is allele 1. Every reader
applies the same rule, so the same VCF gives identical dosage columns from a
file path and from a data frame, tied markers included.

\strong{The \code{alleles} column of HapMap input is checked, not trusted.} If the
calls at a marker contain alleles that are not in its declared pair (a stale
or opposite-strand \code{alleles} column), a warning names the affected rows and the
marker is oriented from the calls; the returned \code{allele} column then shows the
alleles actually observed.

The returned \code{allele} column lists the two alleles as \code{"allele1/allele2"} in
the order above (for a marker whose second allele was never observed, only
allele 1). The column itself records the raw orientation, not which allele
was coded \code{1}.

\strong{Which allele is counted.} The allele coded \code{1} (or \code{2} under
\code{code_as = "012"}) at each marker is recorded in the \code{"counted_allele"}
attribute of the returned data frame, a character vector with one allele per
marker (\code{NA} where it is not known, and absent under \code{model = "Dom"}, which
counts no allele). No dosage value depends on it: it only lets
\code{\link[=cross]{cross()}}, \code{\link[=c.Population]{c.Population()}} and the selection schemes see whether two
separately converted panels count the same allele. Two panels that both
carry it and count different alleles at a marker are refused when crossed or
pooled (e.g. an all-\code{AA} panel and an all-\code{GG} panel would otherwise both
encode \code{+1} and cross to an F1 of \code{+1} instead of the heterozygote \code{0}). The
attribute lives in the R object only: it is not written to a numeric text file,
and it is dropped by row-subsetting the data frame, so numeric data read back
from a file (or created by other software) carries no record and the older
label-only check is used instead; convert panels that will be crossed
\strong{jointly}, or with the same \code{ref_allele}, to be safe.

Calls are matched case-insensitively. A marker with more than two alleles is
set to missing with a warning that counts the affected markers; missing
marker IDs in VCF / PLINK input are filled with \code{chr:pos}, and duplicated
marker IDs or sample names are an error (downstream functions index by name).

\strong{VCF calls.} Only complete, biallelic diploid genotype calls are used, from a
file path and from an in-memory VCF table alike: haploid calls (\code{"0"}, \code{"1"}),
partially missing calls (\code{"./1"}) and calls that name a second ALT allele
(\code{"0/2"}, \code{"2/2"}) are set to missing, and one warning per kind counts them.
The marker's orientation is then chosen from the remaining calls.

Set \code{method = "reference"} with \code{ref_allele} to fix the reference allele
yourself instead — for example to keep the coding consistent with an
external panel, where the major allele in your sample may not be the one you
want coded \code{1}.

\code{code_as = "012"} switches to counting the reference allele (\code{2}/\code{1}/\code{0})
rather than centering on the heterozygote.
}

\section{The output, and the \code{cm} column}{

The result has five metadata columns — \code{snp}, \code{allele}, \code{chr}, \code{pos}, \code{cm} —
followed by one column per individual. The types are the same for every input
format: \code{snp}, \code{allele} and \code{chr} are character, \code{pos} is integer (double only
if a position is not a whole number below 2^31, and integer \code{NA} when the
input carries no positions), \code{cm} is double, and the genotype columns are
integer. Numeric-format input (a data frame or a file written by
\code{as_numeric()}) is brought to this schema too (logical and whole-number double
genotype columns become integer, values unchanged), so converting a written
file again returns the same table.

\code{cm} is the genetic map in centiMorgans, and it is \code{NA} unless the input
format carries one. HapMap and VCF have no genetic-distance field, so \code{cm}
is \code{NA} for them; PLINK \code{.bim} / \code{.map} files do (column 3), and it is kept
when populated. This matters because meiosis needs recombination distances:
\code{\link[=as_population]{as_population()}} and the crossing functions refuse to run on an all-\code{NA}
map. Fill one in from the physical positions when you have no real map:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{num <- as_numeric("my_genotypes.hmp.txt", to_r = TRUE)
num$cm <- synthetic_map(num$chr, num$pos)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Note also that \code{aggregate(cm ~ chr, ...)} returns "no rows to aggregate" on
an all-\code{NA} map, since the formula interface drops missing values.
}

\examples{
# A small HapMap table: 11 metadata columns, then one column per individual.
hmp <- data.frame(
  `rs#`   = c("snp1", "snp2", "snp3"),
  alleles = c("C/T", "A/G", "G/T"),
  chrom   = c(1, 1, 2),
  pos     = c(100, 500, 200),
  strand  = "+", `assembly#` = NA, center = NA,
  protLSID = NA, assayLSID = NA, panelLSID = NA, QCcode = NA,
  L1 = c("C", "A", "G"),
  L2 = c("T", "G", "T"),
  L3 = c("C", "A", "T"),
  L4 = c("T", "G", "G"),
  check.names = FALSE, stringsAsFactors = FALSE
)

# to_r = TRUE returns the result instead of writing it to disk.
num <- as_numeric(hmp, to_r = TRUE, verbose = FALSE)
num

# 0 / 1 / 2 coding instead of -1 / 0 / 1:
as_numeric(hmp, to_r = TRUE, code_as = "012", verbose = FALSE)

# Orient the coding against a reference allele rather than by frequency:
as_numeric(hmp, to_r = TRUE, verbose = FALSE,
           method = "reference", ref_allele = c("C", "A", "G"))

# From a file, the format is detected from its contents:
\dontrun{
num <- as_numeric("my_genotypes.hmp.txt", to_r = TRUE)
num <- as_numeric("my_genotypes.vcf", to_r = TRUE)
}
}
